// rustc borrowck/issue-36082.rs — upstream E0716
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// shelf: fail (was admit, root bck.NEW-BCS; CLOSED 2026-09-14o-autoreffund)
// RE-PORTED AS-IS 2026-09-07 (stage 3). The old port was `let val: &i64 = &mk().a;`
// — a field projection of a temporary in a `let` INITIALIZER, which Rust extends,
// so it was legal and sat in bc_admits_blocked as LEGAL-AS-PORTED. Upstream
// projects OUT OF A `RefCell` GUARD returned by a method call: the guard is a
// temporary dropped at the end of the statement and the borrow outlives it.
// Original path: tests/ui/borrowck/issue-36082.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 9-14 —
//     let val: &_ = x.borrow().0;   //~ ERROR temporary value dropped while borrowed [E0716]
//     println!("{}", val);          //~ NOTE borrow later used here
// Modifications:
//   · `RefCell` is KEPT — it is `logos.lang.cell::RefCell`, the same guard shape
//   · the cell's content `(&mut r, s)` -> a plain `i64`, and the `.0` projection ->
//     `.deref()` on the guard. Upstream's `.0` moves a `&mut` out through the
//     guard, so the result borrows the guard; a copied `&i64` field would not
//     (rustc accepts that twin), and `&*x.borrow()` is lifetime-EXTENDED (rustc
//     accepts it too, measured 2026-10-11). `x.borrow().deref()` is the method-
//     call receiver, which is not extended: rustc E0716, the upstream subject —
//     a reference derived from a guard TEMPORARY, used after the statement in
//     which the guard is dropped. (Was `Ref::get()`, removed 2026-10-11: Rust's
//     guards have no inherent accessor.)
//   · `println!("{}", val)` -> `return *val as i32` (assert -> exit code)
// ⚠ THE `Ref` DROP RUNS: `impl<T> Drop for Ref<T>` decrements the borrow flag, so
// the admitted program also releases the RefCell's borrow state while `val` is live.
package issue_36082;
use logos.lang.cell;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let x: RefCell<i64> = RefCell::new(5i64);
    let val: &i64 = x.borrow().deref();   // refused: upstream E0716
    return *val as i32;
}
